        <qualifiedname>OpenSkyhawk::AxisCal::deadzone</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Reserved, always 0 in blob version 1. The field exists only so that adding a deadzone later needs no CAL_VERSION bump; nothing reads it, and the wire protocol rejects a non-zero value. <ref refid="classOpenSkyhawk_1_1AnalogInput" kindref="compound">AnalogInput</ref>&apos;s 128-count output hysteresis already hides return scatter, and if scatter ever exceeds that the fix is that axis&apos;s <computeroutput>hysteresis</computeroutput> argument, not a user-facing control. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
        <location file="Firmware/Libraries/SimGateway/AxisCal.h" line="52" column="14" bodyfile="Firmware/Libraries/SimGateway/AxisCal.h" bodystart="52" bodyend="-1"/>
      </memberdef>
    </sectiondef>
    <briefdescription>
<para>Captured endpoints for one axis, unsigned 0–65535 throughout. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><computeroutput>centre</computeroutput> is always <emphasis>provided</emphasis> data, never derived — the client captures it, and neither the device nor the client substitutes <computeroutput>(min + max) / 2</computeroutput>. Raw midpoint is not physical midpoint whenever the transfer is nonlinear, and for a rotating-magnet hall axis it is nonlinear by construction (the normal field component goes as sin θ). An offset centre is the normal case and is exactly what the second segment exists to absorb. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
